summary.value:
The paper proposes Uni-Plan, a high-level planning model built on a unified multimodal model BAGEL. Uni-Plan simultaneously functions as a policy, dynamics model, self-discriminator and value function. Experiments on long-horizon planning tasks demonstrate the effectiveness of the approach.

The initial scores from the four reviewers are 2, 4, 4, and 4. The major concerns raised include:
1) missing comparisons with prior planning methods and world-model-based approaches (Lo2k, rs1q);
2) lack of statistical rigor in the experimental results (Lo2k);
3) evaluation on relatively simple tasks with full state observability and limited long-horizon difficulty (VZiD, 1NeT, rs1q);
4) insufficient generalization studies and absence of challenging real-robot experiments (VZiD);
5) lack of comprehensive quantitative analysis across all environments (1NeT).

The rebuttal partially addresses some of these concerns. However, points 1, 3, 5 remain insufficiently resolved in the AC’s assessment. 
The evaluated tasks are not sufficiently challenging and assume full initial observability. While the authors argue that performing open-loop planning in the first place is an advantage, this claim is not well supported, especially for dynamic environments where closed-loop planning is more effective.
Additionally, the ablation studies are limited. For example, it is unclear how the proposed unified model compares against architectures that use separate modules for policy, dynamics, and value estimation, or against established planning methods that leverage explicit world models [1]. 
[1] Wu Y, Tian T, Swamy G, et al. From Foresight to Forethought: VLM-In-the-Loop Policy Steering via Latent Alignment. RSS 2025.

Therefore, the AC recommends rejecting the paper.
